Customer Churn Prediction – Project Analysis
1. Problem Understanding

Customer churn occurs when users stop using a service.
Predicting churn helps businesses identify at-risk customers early and take preventive action such as targeted offers, improved support, or plan adjustments.

This project uses Machine Learning to classify customers as:

Churn = 1 → Likely to leave

Churn = 0 → Likely to stay

2. Dataset Overview

The dataset contains customer information such as:

Demographics (gender, senior citizen, etc.)

Service usage (internet, phone, streaming, etc.)

Contract details (monthly charges, tenure, payment method)

Target variable: Churn

Both numerical and categorical features are present.

3. Data Preprocessing

Several preprocessing steps were required:

3.1 Handling Missing Values

The TotalCharges column contained non-numeric values.
These were converted to numeric and rows with missing values were removed.

3.2 Encoding Categorical Variables

Categorical features were converted into numeric format using One-Hot Encoding so that ML models could process them.

3.3 Feature Scaling

Numerical features were scaled using StandardScaler to improve model performance and training stability.

3.4 Train–Test Split

The data was split into:

80% Training

20% Testing

This ensures unbiased evaluation on unseen data.

4. Machine Learning Models

Two main classification models were trained:

4.1 Logistic Regression

A simple and interpretable baseline model used for binary classification.

4.2 Random Forest

An ensemble model that captures non-linear relationships and feature interactions.

Both models were trained on the same preprocessed data for fair comparison.

5. Model Evaluation

Performance was measured using:

Accuracy

Precision

Recall

F1-score

Confusion Matrix

These metrics help evaluate not just correctness, but how well the model identifies churners specifically (important for business impact).

6. Key Observations

Customers with short tenure showed higher churn rates.

Month-to-month contracts had more churn compared to long-term contracts.

Higher monthly charges were associated with increased churn.

Service-related factors (internet type, support availability) influenced retention.

These insights align with real-world customer behavior patterns.

7. Optional: Neural Network (Basic DL)

A simple Artificial Neural Network (ANN) was implemented using TensorFlow and Keras to explore Deep Learning for classification.

Architecture:

Input layer

Two hidden dense layers (ReLU)

Output layer (Sigmoid)

The ANN achieved comparable performance to traditional ML models, showing that simpler models can often be sufficient for structured tabular data.

8. Conclusion

This project demonstrates an end-to-end Machine Learning workflow:

Data cleaning

Feature engineering

Model training

Performance evaluation

Business interpretation
